From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: SG Defender scans an image before it is mapped

SG Defender's real-time protection on execute (David 2026-11-25, issue #10:
like Windows Defender). Before an image is mapped -- a program being
started, a DLL being loaded -- NtCreateSection(SEC_IMAGE) hands the file to
sg-session's sg-defender service, which asks ClamAV; one that holds malware
gets no section: STATUS_VIRUS_INFECTED (ERROR_VIRUS_INFECTED, 225), as
Windows fails CreateProcess and LoadLibrary for a detection. The service has
kept a copy in its quarantine; the file is removed here, as this user, if it
is still the file that was scanned.

Security: the service is root and is never told a path to open. The file goes
as a new descriptor of this process's own (O_RDONLY, opened through
/proc/self/fd with this process's rights) over SCM_RIGHTS on an AF_UNIX
socket (root's, 0660 to the Windows-side group); the service learns who asks
from SO_PEERCRED and reads only what the caller could read.

Not asked about: a prefix's fake DLLs and its copies of Wine's own builtins
(Wine maps its builtin, never the file), and Wine's installed files (root's,
under lib/wine; a build tree's own). If the service is not there, or does not
answer within 30 s (SG_DEFENDER_TIMEOUT_MS shortens it), the program runs, as
on Windows with Defender off. SG_DEFENDER_SOCKET points elsewhere for tests.

__wine_sg_defender_scan (ntdll export, a unix call with a WoW64 thunk) lets
the installer, the script hosts and AMSI ask about an open file or a text
(9458-9460). Gate test-defender-exec.
